# ==========================================================
# Cell 17 : RANK, ORDER, ASSEMBLE, ANSWER
#
#   score = match quality x hop decay x relationship strength x importance
#   "importance" is either plain degree (default) or PageRank if
#   USE_PAGERANK is turned on below -- see PAGERANK_SCORES.
#
#   ORDERING: after ranking picks WHICH triplets to keep, order_bfs()
#   decides what SEQUENCE to present them in. Saleh et al. (SG-RAG MOT,
#   2025) found that presenting triplets in graph-traversal order (BFS
#   from the seed) measurably outperformed random or score order,
#   because each triplet stays connected to one already seen -- this
#   helps the LLM chain through the context instead of reading
#   disconnected facts ("lost in the middle"). Ranking still decides
#   inclusion; BFS only decides the sequence of what was already kept.
#
#   INLINE-LINK RELEVANCE: Cell 10 skips doc:wiki links when building
#   graph edges (IGNORE_LINK_TYPES), so those relationships never
#   become traversable edges in G -- traverse() can never reach a
#   document through one. But the rendered <Title> hyperlink markers
#   (Cell 8d) are still sitting in each document's own content. This
#   cell recovers that as a SECOND relevance signal in pick_documents:
#   does a document's own inline anchors point at the entities the
#   query resolved to, independent of whether graph traversal ever
#   reached that document. This is additive -- it does not add the
#   missing relationships back into G itself.
#
#   Definitions only. Nothing runs here -- ask a question in a new
#   cell with:  kg_answer("your question")
#   (The inline-anchor cache below is built lazily, on first use inside
#   kg_retrieve/pick_documents -- not at cell-load time -- so this
#   comment is actually true now.)
# ==========================================================
